From: System Electronics
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 11:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/hailo: program the two DPI frame counters one cycle apart

The driver computes frame_cycles and then writes the constant 10000000 to
both DPM_9 and HEADER_4. With the two registers set to the same value the
two frame counters inside the DPI drift one 600 MHz cycle per frame, and
after about 35000 frames the DPI stops feeding the DSI: the display goes
black every 587 s at 60 Hz, and only a restart of the video stream
generator brings it back.

Measured on Astrial H15 with a 1280x800 panel: HEADER_4 one cycle above
DPM_9 keeps the display alive for 5961 s across three windows, 1800 s of
them with a 30 fps stream, and the count resumes exactly where it stopped
when the two values are made equal again. One cycle below halves the time
to failure, 296 s instead of 587 s, which is what an off-by-one predicts.

Use the computed value as well: on this SoC it is exactly 10000000, so no
register bit changes today, but the relation survives a mode change.

Upstream-Status: Pending

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/hailo/hailo_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/hailo/hailo_drv.c
@@ -161,9 +161,12 @@
 	writel(SW_UPDATE_TIME_RANGE((frame_duration_ns * 2) / 3),
 	       priv->regs + DPM_7);
 
-	// frame_cycles = dsi_sys_clk / refresh = 600 MHz / 60 = 10M
-	writel(10000000, priv->regs + DPM_9);
-	writel(10000000, priv->regs + HEADER_4);
+	/*
+	 * HEADER_4 one cycle above DPM_9: with equal values the two frame
+	 * counters slip one cycle per frame and the stream stalls.
+	 */
+	writel(frame_cycles - 1, priv->regs + DPM_9);
+	writel(frame_cycles, priv->regs + HEADER_4);
 
 	writel(CFG_HA_STREAM_START, priv->regs + HA_STREAM_START_EXT_DATA_OUT);
 
